From: Matthew Dowell <mbd06b+github@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 19 Nov 2026 04:56:28 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/6] feat: `brit rev-parse`, Git's spelling of `brit revision
 resolve`

Agents guess `git rev-parse` first and fell back to Git when brit refused the
name. `brit rev-parse` answers the forms the workspace census found, with Git's
output: full object names, `--short[=n]` (which implies `--verify`, as in Git),
`--verify` with `-q`/`--quiet`, `--abbrev-ref`, `A..B` as `B` and `^A`, and the
path queries `--show-toplevel`, `--git-dir`, `--absolute-git-dir`,
`--git-common-dir` and `--path-format=absolute|relative` in command-line order.
`--git-dir` and `--git-common-dir` follow Git's relative/absolute choices in a
main worktree, its subdirectories, linked worktrees and submodules. Failures print
Git's messages with exit 128, or exit 1 silently for `--verify --quiet`.

The arguments are parsed by a hand-written `clap::Args` so that ordering survives
while every flag stays visible to `--cli-surface-json`. The command is read-only and
classified with the other inspection commands in `requires_mutation_guard`.

The journey `tests/cli-journey/tests/rev_parse.rs` compares stdout and exit code
with Git in four directories and checks that the help names `brit revision resolve`.

